ior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": -6.52, "poll_sd": 4.51, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": -0.96, "lean": -5.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": -0.34}, "rating_margin": -10.7, "sigma_race": 4.5, "sigma_total": 5.29, "baseline_margin": -6.65, "analyst_adjustment": 0.15, "key_factors": ["Consistent polling shows Moody ahead by roughly 5-8 points in a state with a Republican lean", "Expert ratings converge on Likely/Solid R, with markets pricing Nixon's chances near 10%", "Nixon has outraised Moody and drawn national attention, but no poll shows her leading", "Moody benefits from incumbency as the appointed sitting senator"], "rationale": "Fourteen recent polls uniformly show Moody ahead, averaging about six points, and ten expert ratings plus prediction markets all point the same direction. Nixon's strong fundraising and Sanders surrogacy give her an underdog storyline, but the evidence supports a Likely Republican win.", "watch": "A late surge among young and Black voters, or a polling miss in a low-turnout special election, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Appointed Senator Ashley Moody, a Republican, faces state Representative Angie Nixon in Florida's special Senate election. Florida's recent partisan lean favors Republicans by about five points, and Moody carries the advantages of incumbency and name recognition from her time as attorney general. Every independent poll since late September shows her ahead, with margins ranging from two to twelve points and an average near six.\n\nNixon has run a well-funded campaign, outraising Moody in the third quarter and drawing national progressive support, including a Bernie Sanders visit. Still, prediction markets give her roughly a one-in-ten chance, and expert raters broadly place the race in Likely Republican territory. A Nixon win would require a substantial polling error or an unusual turnout pattern in a speci